 <h2>What you never have to do</h2>
 <ul>
-    <li>Create the task folder manually — the PreToolUse hook forces it.</li>
-    <li>Remember to add the AI trailers — the commit-msg hook rejects commits without them.</li>
-    <li>Worry about Claude editing files before the folder exists — same hook blocks edits outside <code>ai_coding/active/&lt;task&gt;/</code> until you (or Claude) create the folder.</li>
-    <li>Manually route diffs to task folders — the post-commit hook does it.</li>
+    <li>Create the task folder by hand. A hook stops Claude from editing source files until the folder exists, so Claude makes it.</li>
+    <li>Remember to add the AI tag lines. The local commit hook will reject the commit if they are missing.</li>
+    <li>Worry about Claude editing files before the folder exists. The same hook blocks any edit outside <code>ai_coding/active/&lt;task&gt;/</code> until a task folder is in place.</li>
+    <li>Manually copy diffs into task folders. A post-commit hook does that for you.</li>
 </ul>
 
 <h2>Common edge cases</h2>
 <details>
-    <summary>The CI merge gate failed on my PR</summary>
+    <summary>The CI merge gate failed on my pull request</summary>
     <p>
-        Two causes. (1) A commit on the branch is missing trailers — find
-        the offending SHA in the workflow log, amend or replace that commit.
-        (2) <code>AI-Verdict: CAP_REACHED</code> or <code>OVERRIDE</code>
-        appears on a commit — the gate blocks these so a human reviews them
-        before merge. Decide whether the override is acceptable, then either
-        rewrite history with a passing verdict or merge admin-override.
+        Two common causes. (1) A commit on the branch is missing the AI
+        tag lines. Find the bad commit in the failed check log and either
+        rewrite it or replace it. (2) A commit has
+        <code>AI-Verdict: CAP_REACHED</code> or
+        <code>AI-Verdict: OVERRIDE</code>. The gate blocks these on
+        purpose so a human reviews them first. Decide whether the
+        override is fine. Then either rewrite the history so the verdict
+        is <code>PASS</code>, or merge with an admin override.
     </p>
 </details>
 <details>
-    <summary>I want to work on something small and non-pipeline</summary>
+    <summary>I want to work on something small and skip the pipeline</summary>
     <p>
-        Use a branch name that doesn't match <code>feature/*</code>,
-        <code>fix/*</code>, or <code>task/*</code>, and don't touch
-        <code>ai_coding/active|archive|quarantine/</code>. The gate will
-        exit silently and your PR goes through normal review. Note: the
-        PreToolUse hook still blocks Claude from editing source files
-        without a task folder — pass <code>--no-verify</code> on local
-        commits if you want to bypass the commit-msg hook for non-pipeline
-        work too.
+        Use a branch name that does not start with <code>feature/</code>,
+        <code>fix/</code>, or <code>task/</code>. Do not add anything
+        under <code>ai_coding/active</code>,
+        <code>ai_coding/archive</code>, or
+        <code>ai_coding/quarantine</code>. The merge gate will skip the
+        check and your pull request goes through normal review. The
+        Claude Code hooks still block edits to source files until a task
+        folder exists, so to bypass the local commit hook on a quick
+        commit, use <code>git commit --no-verify</code>.
     </p>
 </details>
